In .NET 10 / MAUI 10, `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` no longer works because Android deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` and MAUI removed support for it.

**The fix:** Use the new `KeyboardAcceleratorsMode` or, more specifically, set your Android window's soft input mode to `AdjustPan` and restructure your layout to work with the system's edge-to-edge insets.

### Recommended approach

**1. In your `MauiProgram.cs` or page handler, use `AdjustPan`:**

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
    Window.SetSoftInputMode(Android.Views.SoftInput.AdjustPan);
}
```

**2. Better: Use `Keyboard.AccelerateLayout` (MAUI 10's replacement):**

In .NET 10, wrap your bottom input in a layout that responds to keyboard insets:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />

    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

Then in your page or `App.xaml.cs`, opt into the new behavior:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
{
#if ANDROID
    Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.WindowHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping(
        "KeyboardInsets", (handler, view) =>
        {
            var activity = handler.PlatformView;
            activity.Window?.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false);
            activity.Window?.SetSoftInputMode(
                Android.Views.SoftInput.AdjustResize); // Works with edge-to-edge
        });
#endif
});
```

**3. Enable edge-to-edge + inset handling (the proper .NET 10 way):**

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    // Edge-to-edge mode makes AdjustResize work again via insets
    WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
        Window.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!,
        new InsetsListener());
}

class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
```

### TL;DR

| .NET 9 | .NET 10 |
|--------|---------|
| `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` | Use `WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)` + IME inset listener |

Option 3 (edge-to-edge + `OnApplyWindowInsets`) is the most robust — it's what Google now recommends and what MAUI 10 aligns with. Your bottom input bar will slide up exactly by the keyboard height.
